'''brake<sup>3</sup>''' (brāk), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''braked'', ppr. ''braking''. [= MLG. LG. D. ''braken'' (> F. ''braquer'') = Sw. ''bråka'' = Dan. ''brage'', brake; from the noun. Cf. ''break'', ''v.''] '''1†.''' To crack or break (the stalks of flax) in order to separate the woody portions from the fiber. Now written ''break''.
{{smaller block|It [flax] must be watered, dried, ''braked'', tew-tawed, and with much labor driuen and reduced in the end to be as soft and tender as wooll. {{right|''Holland'', tr. of Pliny, xix. (proem).}}}}
'''2.''' To retard or stop the motion of by the application of a brake.

